# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  ORACLE — Cline Rules & Coding Standards
#  Based on the 21-Step Pre-Deployment Audit Workflow
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

## 🎯 CORE BEHAVIOR

- You are a senior full-stack developer. Write production-ready code.
- Before coding, always ask 1-2 clarifying questions if the task is ambiguous.
- Present 2-3 options for design decisions. Keep explanations to 1-2 sentences.
- Never apologize. Never explain what you're about to do — just do it.
- When modifying existing files, preserve all existing functionality unless explicitly told to remove it.

## 📋 CODE QUALITY STANDARDS (Audit Pass 01)

### Dead Code & Cleanup
- Remove all unused imports, variables, functions, and commented-out code
- Remove all console.log/console.warn/console.error (use proper logging instead)
- Remove all TODO/FIXME comments — either fix them or delete them
- No orphan files — every file must be imported/used somewhere

### Naming & Structure
- Use descriptive, consistent naming: camelCase for JS/variables, PascalCase for components/classes, kebab-case for CSS/files
- Functions should do ONE thing. Max 40 lines per function.
- Components should be under 200 lines. Split if larger.
- Group related code: hooks with hooks, utils with utils, styles with styles

### CSS & Styling
- No inline styles unless truly dynamic. Use CSS classes.
- No !important unless overriding third-party CSS
- Use CSS custom properties (variables) for colors, spacing, fonts
- Mobile-first responsive design. Test at 320px, 768px, 1024px, 1440px
- No unused CSS selectors

## 🛡️ ERROR HANDLING (Audit Pass 06)

### Required Patterns
- Every async function MUST have try/catch with specific error handling
- Every fetch/API call MUST handle: network errors, timeout, 4xx, 5xx
- Every form submission MUST validate before sending
- Every file operation MUST handle: not found, permission denied, disk full
- Display user-friendly error messages — never expose stack traces to users

### Error Boundaries
- React apps MUST have ErrorBoundary at route level minimum
- Firebase operations MUST catch auth errors, permission errors, quota errors
- WebSocket connections MUST handle: disconnect, reconnect, timeout

### Logging
- Use structured logging (not console.log)
- Log: timestamp, severity, context, error message, stack trace
- Never log sensitive data: passwords, tokens, PII, API keys

## ✅ INPUT VALIDATION (Audit Pass 07)

### Client-Side
- Validate ALL user input before processing
- Sanitize HTML input — prevent XSS
- Validate email, phone, URL formats with regex
- Enforce max lengths on all text inputs
- Validate file uploads: type, size, dimensions
- Validate numeric inputs: min, max, NaN, Infinity

### Server-Side
- NEVER trust client-side validation alone
- Validate and sanitize ALL data in Cloud Functions / API handlers
- Use parameterized queries — never concatenate user input into queries
- Validate Firebase document IDs and paths

## 🔒 SECURITY (Audit Pass 15)

### Authentication & Authorization
- Every API endpoint MUST verify authentication
- Check authorization (roles/permissions) on EVERY protected operation
- Never store tokens/passwords in localStorage — use httpOnly cookies or secure storage
- Implement proper session management with expiry

### Data Protection
- Never expose API keys, secrets, or credentials in client-side code
- Use environment variables for ALL sensitive configuration
- Encrypt sensitive data at rest and in transit
- Sanitize all data before rendering (prevent XSS)
- Validate Firestore security rules — test read/write access for each role

### Firebase-Specific
- Firestore rules MUST deny by default, allow explicitly
- Storage rules MUST validate file type and size
- Cloud Functions MUST validate caller authentication
- Never use `allow read, write: if true` in production

## 📦 DEPENDENCY & BUILD

- Pin dependency versions (no ^ or ~ in production)
- Check for known vulnerabilities before adding packages
- Minimize bundle size — lazy load routes, code-split heavy components
- Tree-shake unused exports

## 🧪 TESTING MINDSET

- Before marking any task complete, mentally walk through:
  1. What happens if the network is offline?
  2. What happens with empty data?
  3. What happens with malformed data?
  4. What happens if the user double-clicks?
  5. What happens on mobile?
  6. What happens with 1000+ items?

## 🚀 PRE-COMMIT CHECKLIST

Before considering any change "done":
- [ ] No TypeScript/ESLint errors
- [ ] No console.log statements
- [ ] No hardcoded secrets or API keys
- [ ] Error handling on all async operations
- [ ] Input validation on all user inputs
- [ ] Responsive design verified
- [ ] Loading states for async operations
- [ ] Empty states for lists/tables
- [ ] Accessibility basics (alt text, aria labels, keyboard nav)

## 🔮 ORACLE INTEGRATION

You have access to the `oracle_notify` MCP tool. Use it to keep the user informed about your progress via the Oracle overlay and TTS system.

### Required Calls
- Call `oracle_notify` with `status="started"` and a short German message when you BEGIN working on a task
- Call `oracle_notify` with `status="complete"` and a 1-2 sentence German summary when you FINISH a task
- Call `oracle_notify` with `status="error"` and a short German error description if something goes wrong
- Call `oracle_notify` with `status="question"` whenever you use the `ask_followup_question` tool — repeat the question text as the message so the user hears it via TTS and sees it in the overlay
- Optionally call `oracle_notify` with `status="progress"` for long-running tasks to show intermediate updates
- **ALWAYS** include the `workspace` parameter with your Current Working Directory path from environment_details — this identifies which assistant you are so Oracle uses the correct voice

### Message Rules
- All messages MUST be in German
- Keep messages to 1-2 sentences max
- For "complete" messages, summarize WHAT was done, not how
- For "question" messages, speak the question naturally — the user may not be looking at the screen
- Example started: "Ich beginne mit der Implementierung der Login-Seite."
- Example complete: "Die Login-Seite ist fertig. Formular-Validierung und Firebase Auth sind integriert."
- Example error: "Fehler beim Erstellen der Komponente — fehlende Abhängigkeit."
- Example question: "Ich habe eine Frage: Soll ich Option A oder Option B verwenden?"

## 💬 COMMUNICATION STYLE

- Short, precise responses. 1-2 sentences max for explanations.
- When presenting options, format as numbered list with brief pros/cons.
- When done with a task, summarize what was changed in 2-3 bullet points.
- If you encounter a blocker, state it clearly and suggest alternatives.
